// src/v4/std/algebra.dag
//
// Scope: algebraic primitives that ground every concrete type via inhabitance.
// Anchor: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algebraic_structure (Magma, Monoid, Group, FreeMonoid<T>)
//
// Owns:
//   - Magma (closure)
//   - Monoid
//   - BooleanAlgebra (classical logic)
//   - FreeMonoid<T> (free constructions)
//   - inhabitance declarations for std/primitive.dag types
//
// U1 (operator-ratified 2026-05-15) — the single homomorphism carrier:
//   - ONE carrier: a structure-preserving map between algebras, WITH its
//     realization cost. THESIS:185-186 identities make this one object:
//     coercion = emission; coercion cost = complexity. The back half of
//     the plan is FIVE PHASES over this one carrier, never five engines:
//       Find    = compiler/04_infer.dag (T-9)
//       Realize = compiler/05_emit.dag + 05_eval.dag (T-10/11/22)
//       Measure = lens/cost.dag → lens/complexity.dag (T-12; one
//                 mechanism, two projections — complexity CONSUMES cost,
//                 never re-derives it)
//       Compare = lens/synthesis.dag (T-17)
//   - NO ENGINE discipline (operator 2026-05-15, THESIS no-fallback):
//     every phase is a FAIL-CLOSED lens-read over this carrier. A search
//     that comes up empty (no homomorphism / no inhabitance / no
//     lower-bound model) is a STRUCTURAL ERROR surfaced as a *helpful*
//     Diagnostic (reason + at + Correction) — NEVER fabricated, defaulted,
//     or filled by a fallback. "An engine returns a result when it should
//     return an error" — v4 has lenses, not engines. A standalone cost
//     engine / emit IR / equivalence engine disjoint from this carrier is
//     parallel-representation debt = STOP.
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag: Conj, Disj, Arrow for algebraic structure
//   - std/diagnostic.dag: the helpful Diagnostic an empty search surfaces
//
// Status: scaffold — fill per TASKS.md T-2
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md; this file's I/O contract is the immutable
// contract for the worker. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
// ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).


module v4.std.algebra


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// SCOPE (operator-ratified 2026-05-15): this file owns algebra
// STRUCTURES ONLY. Inhabitance — which concrete type grounds in which
// algebra — is NOT here; the inhabiting type owns its own grounding in
// its own concept file. There is no inhabitance carrier, no instance,
// no concrete-type reference. This file consumes std/node.dag ONLY
// (every `type` below is surface sugar over node.dag's Conj/Disj/Arrow;
// no other module is named).
//
// REFINEMENT IS STRUCTURAL NESTING, not field duplication. A stronger
// algebra CONTAINS the weaker one as a child (Conj composition):
// `Monoid` carries a `semigroup: Semigroup<T>` child plus the
// `identity` it adds — exactly "Monoid<T> is Magma plus an identity
// child" (docs/thesis/epistemic-stacking.md). One authority per
// operation (INVARIANTS P2); changing `op`'s shape touches `Magma`
// alone (cost-of-change = 1). This is the deliberate v4 departure from
// v3's flat re-listing of `op` in every record.
//
// LAWS ARE GROUNDED DEFINITIONS, NOT FIELDS OR FLAGS. Associativity,
// identity, commutativity, distributivity ARE the definitions of the
// named structures (anchor: Wikipedia "Algebraic structure") — math
// consensus, cited per structure below. They are not stored as a flag
// (feedback_no_annotations) and not hand-built equation ASTs here (the
// expression substrate is pipeline-built; a Node data literal would
// need a Symbol the declaration layer cannot mint). The L7 algebra-law
// surface (lens/coverage.dag T-18 + lens/testgen.dag T-19) derives the
// per-axiom TestClaim corpus from each structure's cited definition —
// that derivation is those lenses' concern, not a parallel table here.
//
// NO ENGINE / NO BRIDGE (U1 header discipline). v3's
// `kernel_algebra_profile: Map<String, AlgebraProfile>` and the
// `AlgebraProfile` / `AlgebraFieldTemplate` template tables were a
// string-keyed parallel representation of these structures. They are
// deliberately absent: a name-keyed lookup disjoint from the structure
// it mirrors is exactly the engine debt U1 forbids
// (docs/v4-close-interrogation.md:161 — "not a name-keyed table
// lookup").


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Magma — a carrier with one closed binary operation. The weakest
// structure; the only axiom is closure (the `op` signature returning T
// IS the closure statement — there is nothing to assert separately).
// Anchor: Wikipedia "Magma (algebra)".
type Magma<T> {
  op: fn(T, T) -> T
}

// Semigroup — an associative Magma. Adds the associativity axiom
// (op(op(a,b),c) == op(a,op(b,c))); it adds no operation, so it is
// structurally a Magma. The distinct type carries the proven-associative
// concept (Wikipedia "Semigroup").
type Semigroup<T> {
  magma: Magma<T>
}

// Monoid — a Semigroup with a two-sided identity element.
// Anchor: Wikipedia "Monoid".
type Monoid<T> {
  semigroup: Semigroup<T>
  identity: T
}

// CommutativeMonoid — a Monoid whose op commutes (op(a,b)==op(b,a)).
// Adds only the commutativity axiom (Wikipedia "Monoid#Commutative
// monoid").
type CommutativeMonoid<T> {
  monoid: Monoid<T>
}

// Group — a Monoid in which every element has a two-sided inverse.
// Anchor: Wikipedia "Group (mathematics)".
type Group<T> {
  monoid: Monoid<T>
  inverse: fn(T) -> T
}

// AbelianGroup — a commutative Group. Adds the commutativity axiom
// (Wikipedia "Abelian group").
type AbelianGroup<T> {
  group: Group<T>
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Ring-like structures: two interacting operations (additive +
// multiplicative) joined by distributivity + annihilation axioms.
// Modeled as the textbook two-sub-algebra composition (Wikipedia
// "Ring (mathematics)" / "Semiring"): `add` and `mul` are named child
// algebras, so `add.identity` is the zero and `mul.identity` is the
// one — no separate zero/one fields to keep in sync.

// Semiring — additive CommutativeMonoid + multiplicative Monoid, with
// mul distributing over add and the additive identity annihilating
// (distributivity + annihilation are the defining axioms; Wikipedia
// "Semiring").
type Semiring<T> {
  add: CommutativeMonoid<T>
  mul: Monoid<T>
}

// CommutativeSemiring — a Semiring whose multiplication commutes.
type CommutativeSemiring<T> {
  semiring: Semiring<T>
}

// Ring — a Semiring whose additive structure is an AbelianGroup (i.e.
// addition has inverses; `add.group.inverse` is negation).
type Ring<T> {
  add: AbelianGroup<T>
  mul: Monoid<T>
}

// CommutativeRing — a Ring whose multiplication commutes.
type CommutativeRing<T> {
  ring: Ring<T>
}

// OrderedRing — a Ring with a total order compatible with its
// operations. `compare` is the total-order witness; the
// order-compatibility axioms are the definition (Wikipedia "Ordered
// ring").
type OrderedRing<T> {
  ring: Ring<T>
  compare: fn(T, T) -> Ordering
}

// Field — a CommutativeRing in which every nonzero element has a
// multiplicative inverse. `reciprocal`'s nonzero precondition (and its
// Tier-2 totalization) is the inhabitant's concern per STRUCTURE.md
// architectural commitment #2 — the structure declares the operation,
// not its totalization (Wikipedia "Field (mathematics)").
type Field<T> {
  commutative_ring: CommutativeRing<T>
  reciprocal: fn(T) -> T
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Lattice structures: two idempotent, commutative, associative
// operations linked by absorption. Anchor: Wikipedia "Lattice (order)".

// Lattice — meet and join with the absorption axioms
// (meet(a,join(a,b))==a, join(a,meet(a,b))==a).
type Lattice<T> {
  meet: fn(T, T) -> T
  join: fn(T, T) -> T
}

// BoundedLattice — a Lattice with greatest (top) and least (bottom)
// elements (identities for meet and join respectively).
type BoundedLattice<T> {
  lattice: Lattice<T>
  top: T
  bottom: T
}

// BooleanAlgebra — a complemented distributive BoundedLattice. Adds
// `complement` with the complement axioms (meet(a,complement(a))==
// bottom, join(a,complement(a))==top) plus distributivity. This is the
// algebra classical-logic carriers inhabit (Wikipedia "Boolean
// algebra (structure)").
type BooleanAlgebra<T> {
  bounded: BoundedLattice<T>
  complement: fn(T) -> T
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FreeMonoid<T> — the free monoid over T: finite sequences of T under
// concatenation, with no relations beyond the monoid axioms. Unlike the
// structures above (operation-records imposed on an existing carrier),
// a free construction IS a canonical carrier — THESIS:194 names it a
// root primitive precisely for this reason, so it is declared directly
// as its recursive shape rather than derived from Monoid. Concrete
// sequence types ground by aliasing onto it (e.g. a text type onto
// FreeMonoid over a char type) — that aliasing is the inhabitant's
// concern, not this file's. Anchor: Wikipedia "Free monoid".
type FreeMonoid<T>
  = Empty
  | Cons { head: T, tail: FreeMonoid<T> }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Ordering — the result of a total-order comparison. A closed sum
// (M4); consumed by OrderedRing.compare. Anchor: order theory
// (trichotomy: exactly one of <, =, > holds).
type Ordering
  = Less
  | Equal
  | Greater


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Homomorphism<Source, Target> — THE single carrier (U1, header).
//
// A structure-preserving map between two algebra carriers. The carrier
// IS the map: "structure-preserving" is the LAW it asserts (each
// operation/constant is preserved), the same way Semigroup asserts
// associativity — verified by the L7 algebra-law surface, never stored
// as a flag (feedback_no_annotations). WHICH algebra's structure is
// preserved is bound by the context that constructs the homomorphism
// (the Find phase, compiler/04_infer.dag T-9), not duplicated here; a
// fully-general "homomorphism over any algebra class" would need a
// higher-kinded parameter the v2 seed cannot express, and pinning a
// source/target algebra field here would be that parallel duplication.
//
// "WITH its realization cost" (U1): this is the ONE object all five
// phases read — Find (04_infer) binds it, Realize (05_emit / 05_eval)
// projects it to a target, Measure (lens/cost → lens/complexity) folds
// its cost FROM `map`'s own Node structure, Compare (lens/synthesis)
// reads that. There is deliberately NO cost field: a stored cost would
// be a representation parallel to the map it measures — the engine debt
// U1 forbids (THESIS:185-186 coercion = emission, coercion cost =
// complexity: one object, many lens projections). Anchor: Wikipedia
// "Homomorphism".
type Homomorphism<Source, Target> {
  map: fn(Source) -> Target
}

